.TH NEQN 1
.SH NAME
neqn, checkeq \- typeset mathematics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B neqn
[
.BR \-d xy
] [
.BR \-s n
] [
.BR \-f n
] [ file ] ...
.br
.B checkeq
[ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B neqn
is an
.BR nroff (1)
preprocessor.
(For a tutorial on
.BR neqn ,
see
``Mathematics Typesetting Program'' in the
.IR "Document Processing Guide" .)
Usage is almost always
.IP
neqn file ... | nroff
.PP
If no files are specified,
.B neqn
reads from the standard input.
A line
beginning with `.EQ' marks the start of an equation; the end of an equation
is marked by a line beginning with `.EN'.
Neither of these lines is
altered, so they may be defined in macro packages to get centering,
numbering, etc.
It is also possible to set two characters as `delimiters';
subsequent text between delimiters is also treated as
.B neqn
input.
Delimiters may be set to characters
.I x
and
.I y
with the command-line argument
.BI \-d xy
or (more commonly) with `delim
.IR xy '
between .EQ and .EN.
The
left and right delimiters may be identical.
Delimiters are turned off by
`delim off'.
All text that is neither between delimiters nor between .EQ
and .EN is passed through untouched.
.PP
The program
.B checkeq
reports missing or unbalanced delimiters and
\&.EQ/.EN pairs.
.PP
Tokens within
.B neqn
are separated by spaces, tabs, newlines, braces, double
quotes, tildes or circumflexes.
Braces {} are used for grouping; generally
speaking, anywhere a single character like
.I x
could appear, a complicated
construction enclosed in braces may be used instead.
Tilde ~
represents a full space in the output, circumflex ^ half as much.
.PP
Subscripts and superscripts are produced with the keywords
.B sub
and
.BR sup .
Thus
.I x
.B sub
.I i
makes x_i, 
.I a
.B sub
.I i
.B sup
.I 2
produces a_i^2, and
.I e
.B sup
.RI { x
.B sup
.I 2
+
.I y
.B sup
gives e^(x^2+y^2).
.PP
Fractions are made with
.BR over :
.I a
.B over
.I b
yields a/b.
.PP
.B sqrt
makes square roots:
.I 1
.B "over sqrt"
.RI { "ax " "\fBsup\fP" " 2 +bx+c" }
results in 1/sqrt(ax^2+bx+c).
.PP
The keywords
.B from
and
.B to
introduce lower and upper limits on arbitrary
things: lim(n->inf) sum(0..n) x_i is made with
\fBlim from {\fIn\fB\-> \fIinf\fB } sum from \fI0\fB to \fIn x\fR
\fBsub \fIi\fR.
.PP
Left and right brackets, braces, etc., of the right height are made with
.B left
and
.BR right :
\fBleft [ \fIx \fBsup \fI2 \fB+ \fIy \fBsup \fI2 \fBover \fIalpha \fBright ]\fR ~=~\fI1\fR
produces [x^2 + y^2/alpha] = 1.
The
.B right
clause is optional.
Legal characters after
.B left
and
.B right
are braces, brackets, bars,
.B c
and
.B f
for ceiling and floor, and ""
for nothing at all (useful for a right-side-only bracket).
.PP
Vertical piles of things are made with
.BR pile ,
.BR lpile ,
.BR cpile ,
and
.BR rpile :
\fBpile {\fIa \fBabove \fIb \fBabove \fIc\fB}\fR
produces a pile of
.IR a ,
.IR b ,
.IR c .
There can be an arbitrary number of elements
in a pile.
.B lpile
left-justifies,
.B pile
and
.B cpile
center, with different
vertical spacing, and
.B rpile
right justifies.
.PP
Matrices are made with
.BR matrix :
\fBmatrix { lcol { \fIx \fBsub \fIi \fBabove \fIy \fBsub \fI2 \fB}
ccol { \fI1 \fBabove \fI2 \fB} }\fR
produces a matrix with x_i and y_2 in the first column
and 1 and 2 in the second.
In addition, there is
.B rcol
for a
right-justified column.
.PP
Diacritical marks are made with
.BR dot ,
.BR dotdot ,
.BR hat ,
.BR tilde ,
.BR bar ,
.BR vec ,
.BR dyad ,
and
.BR under :
\fIx \fBdot \fR= \fIf(t) \fBbar\fR is x-dot = f(t)-bar,
\fIy \fBdotdot bar \fR~=~ \fIn \fBunder\fR is
y-dotdot-bar = n-under,
and \fIx \fBvec \fR~=~ \fIy \fBdyad\fR is x-vec = y-dyad.
.PP
Fonts can be changed with
.BR roman ,
.BR italic ,
.BR bold ,
and
.B font
.IR n .
Fonts can be
changed globally in a document by
.B gfont
.IR n ,
or by the command-line
arguments
.BI \-s n
and
.BI \-f n.
Note that
.B nroff
will try to underline anything
in italic fonts, and send bold-on/bold-off codes (if appropriate) to get
typewriter hardware to do bold font.
.PP
Successive display arguments can be lined up.
Place
.B mark
before the
desired lineup point in the first equation; place
.B lineup
at the place that is
to line up vertically in subsequent equations.
.PP
Shorthands may be defined, or existing keywords redefined, with
.BR define :
.B define
.I thing
%
.I replacement
% defines a new token called
.I thing
which
will be replaced by
.I replacement
whenever it appears thereafter.
The %
may be any character that does not occur in
.IR replacement .
.PP
Keywords like
.B sum
(sigma)
.B int
(integral)
.B inf
(infinity) and shorthands like >= (greater or equal) and
!= (not equal) are recognized.
Greek letters are spelled out in the desired case,
as in
.B alpha
or
.BR GAMMA .
Mathematical words like sin, cos, log are made
Roman automatically.
.BR nroff (1)
four-character escapes like \e(dd (double dagger) can
be used anywhere.
Strings enclosed in double quotes "..." are passed
through untouched; this permits keywords to be entered as text, and can
be used to communicate with
.B nroff
when all else fails.
.SH EXAMPLE
To process equations inside the file
.IR report ,
and print out the result:
.IP
neqn report | nroff \-T450 | lpr
.PP
Note that
.B nroff
must be used with the
.B \-T
option.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
nroff(1), tbl(1)
.br
``Mathematics Typesetting Program'' and the ``Nroff User's Manual'' in
the
.IR "Document Processing Guide" .
.SH BUGS
.B neqn
does its best, but depending on the hardware, certain constructions
may not be entirely aesthetically pleasing.
.PP
To embolden digits, parens, etc., it is necessary to quote them, as in
`bold "12.3"'.
.SH "SPECIAL CHARACTER DEFINITIONS"
The following
.B nroff
character definitions, found in file
.BR /usr/pub/eqnchar ,
are for constructing characters that are not available
on printers.
These definitions are primarily intended for use with
.BR neqn .
The command usage is
.IP
\fBneqn /usr/pub/eqnchar\fR [ files ] | \fBnroff\fR [ options ]
.PP
.B eqnchar
contains definitions for the following characters:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta 1.4i 2.8i
\fIciplus\fR	\fI||\fR	\fIsquare\fR
\fIcitimes\fR	\fIlangle\fR	\fIcircle\fR
\fIwig\fR	\fIrangle\fR	\fIblot\fR
\fI\-wig\fR	\fIhbar\fR	\fIbullet\fR
\fI>wig\fR	\fIppd\fR	\fIprop\fR
\fI<wig\fR	\fI<\->\fR	\fIempty\fR
\fI=wig\fR	\fI<=>\fR	\fImember\fR
\fIstar\fR	\fI|<\fR	\fInomem\fR
\fIbigstar\fR	\fI|>\fR	\fIcup\fR
\fI=dot\fR	\fIang\fR	\fIcap\fR
\fIorsign\fR	\fIrang\fR	\fIincl\fR
\fIandsign\fR	\fI3dot\fR	\fIsubset\fR
\fI=del\fR	\fIthf\fR	\fIsupset\fR
